% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/miteh_UNISON-VER_FINAL.pdf Unison with lead-sheet chord markings (MusiCounts)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/miteh_SA-VER_FINAL.pdf Two-part (SA) version (MusiCounts)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/miteh_FourPart-VER_FINAL.pdf Four-part (SATB) version (MusiCounts)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/miteh_Pronuniciation-Guide_FINAL.pdf Pronunciation guide: Woodland Cree translation and SRO Y-dialect by Bill and Emily Sewepagaham
% performance-note: The MusiCounts page embeds an instructional video; use it there. Classes entering the 2026 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge post their own performances through CBC Music; do not copy one here.
% dossier-function: A new choral song in Woodland Cree, commissioned for the 2026 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge (powered by MusiCounts) as that year's Indigenous-language song. MusiCounts describes it as treating the heart as a place of connection to oneself, to others and to community, moving between thought and feeling and back to empathy, care and shared understanding.
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here. MusiCounts offers four arrangements: simplified unison with optional Orff accompaniment, unison with lead-sheet chord markings, two-part SA and four-part SATB; the text has verses, a one-word chorus ("mitêh", heart) and a spoken passage. Read the key, range and form from the score the class will use.
% dossier-practice: Learn the words from the composer's pronunciation guide (Woodland Cree, Y-dialect, Standard Roman Orthography) and the instructional video, not from a reading by ear. The guide's notes on unaspirated K, T and P, the breathed H and the short I are the points to drill. The unison-with-Orff score is the one for the younger grades.
% dossier-accompaniment: The page says "schools are invited to create their own instrumental accompaniment if they choose", and one score includes optional Orff parts. The Band plays nothing for this record. Do not add a hand drum, rattle or other Indigenous instrument: the MusiCounts guide Singing in Indigenous Languages asks that such instruments be left to an Indigenous student or Knowledge Keeper.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the composer's, in Woodland Cree with English translations in the guide; they are not copied here. The translation and orthography are credited to Bill and Emily Sewepagaham of the Little Red River Cree Nation. Sing all of it as printed; no verse is left out.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a 2026 song by a living Cree-Dene composer, not traditional. Nothing here is ours; the record only links to it.
% dossier-licence: Terms as stated: the page heading reads "A new, open-access choral work by Sherryl Sewepagaham", and "Free, downloadable scores are available in SATB, SA, Unison, and Unison + Orff arrangements". The site footer reads "© 2024 Music Education Charity of Canada (MECC)", and its Terms & Conditions cover only applications and submissions. The scores print the title, the composer, "Edited by Kimberly Denis" and "Commissioned for the 2026 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge, Powered by MusiCounts", with no licence. So "open access" here means free to download, learn and perform (and to enter the Challenge), not a licence to repost or rework the score. Link only. Credit wording to use: "mitêh (Heart) by Sherryl Sewepagaham (Woodland Cree-Dene, Little Red River Cree Nation), edited by Kimberly Denis; Cree translation and pronunciation by Bill and Emily Sewepagaham. Commissioned for the 2026 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge, powered by MusiCounts. musicounts.ca/en/miteh" (to confirm with the composer).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary song shared by its Cree composer for teaching, not a ceremonial one. Tell its story (why it was written, what the words say) before singing it, credit the composer and the translators every time, and do not add drums or rattles. The composer is also the author of the Orff Canada article on Indigenous music this workshop follows.
% dossier-review: Sherryl Sewepagaham (the composer) for the credit and any posting; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. Neither has reviewed it.
% lesson: Before the lesson: download the score for your grade from musicounts.ca/en/miteh (Simplified Unison with optional Orff for the younger grades; Unison with chords, SA or SATB for older ones) and the pronunciation guide. Check the range on the score against your class.
% lesson: Tell the story first: Sherryl Sewepagaham is a Cree-Dene singer, composer and music teacher from the Little Red River Cree Nation in northern Alberta, and she wrote mitêh for the 2026 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge. The song is about the heart as a place of connection: to yourself, to others and to your community. Name Bill and Emily Sewepagaham, who wrote the Cree translation and pronunciation.
% lesson: Listen and watch: play the instructional video on the MusiCounts page once through without singing.
% lesson: Pronunciation: from the guide, practise the sounds that trip English speakers: K, T and P with no puff of air, H as a strong breath, and I as in "fit", never "eat". Say the title as the guide gives it, then speak each verse line in rhythm, with the guide's phonetic line projected beside the score.
% lesson: Sing from the composer's score: the chorus first (the single word mitêh), then the verses one at a time, then the whole song. Older classes add the second part (SA) once the unison is secure.
% lesson: Accompaniment: MusiCounts invites schools to make their own instrumental accompaniment; the Unison + Orff score already has parts for barred instruments. Use pitched classroom instruments only, no hand drum or rattle (the guide Singing in Indigenous Languages, p. 6).
% lesson: The spoken passage: read the guide's English meaning together and talk about what it asks of the singers before speaking it in Cree.
% lesson: Close: name the composer and the translators again. If the class enters the Class Challenge, follow CBC's entry rules; otherwise keep any recording inside the school.
